AIPRO

AI Digital Human Platform Operating Through an Authorized Real-Person Identity

First Developer: Yingzhi Zhao (赵颖知)
Second Developer: James Feng (詹老师)

Repository:
https://github.com/James19890801/feishu-codex-digital-employee


PRODUCT SUMMARY

AIPRO is a local-first, multi-channel AI digital human platform for Feishu/Lark,
DingTalk, WeCom, and personal WeChat. It is designed for an account owner who wants an AI system
to receive work-related messages, understand requests, use approved knowledge
and tools, and reply through an explicitly authorized enterprise IM channel.

Feishu remains the protected primary channel. Its core text path uses official
user OAuth and the official lark-cli tool. Incoming Feishu messages are
discovered primarily through user-visible message polling, with a WebSocket
event consumer as a secondary low-latency signal. DingTalk uses the official
DingTalk Workspace CLI personal-event WebSocket and current-user sending APIs.
WeCom uses the official Tencent WeCom AI Bot WebSocket SDK. Personal WeChat is
an optional integration through the third-party GeWe REST API and public
Webhook service. GeWe is not a Tencent official WeChat Open API.

Identity modes are intentionally explicit:

    Feishu: authorized real-person user identity
    DingTalk: authorized real-person user identity
    WeCom: official AI Bot identity
    Personal WeChat: authorized personal account through a third-party provider

AIPRO never presents the WeCom bot channel as a real-person identity. Each
optional channel has an isolated connection lifecycle and health state. A
missing credential, expired login, network failure, or reconnect loop in
DingTalk, WeCom, or GeWe cannot stop the Feishu polling loop or Codex response
engine. Personal WeChat is disabled by default.

The platform combines:

    Real-person identity messaging
    Multi-channel message routing
    Group mention and direct-message triggers
    Pluggable AI coding runtime for understanding and response generation
    Persistent message queues
    Message deduplication
    Per-conversation ordering
    Retry and dead-letter handling
    Local conversation memory
    Structured audit records
    Rate limiting
    Human takeover controls
    Feishu document, task, calendar, image, and file integrations
    A local operations dashboard
    macOS process supervision and recovery


SUPPORTED IM CHANNELS

Feishu / Lark

    Role: protected primary channel
    Identity: authorized user
    Inbound: user-visible message polling plus auxiliary WebSocket
    Group trigger: explicit mention of the account owner
    Direct trigger: every qualifying inbound direct message
    Outbound: official lark-cli user-identity commands
    AI label: disabled when digitalTwinLabel is empty

DingTalk

    Role: optional channel
    Identity: currently authorized DingTalk user
    Required runtime: dingtalk-workspace-cli 1.0.55 or later
    Inbound group trigger: user_im_message_receive_at
    Inbound direct trigger: user_im_message_receive_o2o_all
    Outbound: dws chat message send
    AI label: explicitly disabled with --ai-tag=false
    Delivery idempotency: dws --uuid
    Transport: managed personal-event WebSocket with automatic supervision

WeCom

    Role: optional channel
    Identity: official WeCom AI Bot
    SDK: @wecom/aibot-node-sdk 1.0.7 or later
    Inbound: official WebSocket message callbacks
    Group and direct messages: supported by the configured AI Bot
    Outbound: official WebSocket sendMessage API
    Transport: heartbeat, authentication, and reconnect support from the
    official Tencent WeCom SDK

Personal WeChat through GeWe

    Role: optional, disabled-by-default channel
    Identity: the personal WeChat account explicitly authorized by its owner
    Provider: GeWe third-party API; not a Tencent official Open API
    Inbound: public HTTPS Webhook forwarded to a loopback-only local receiver
    Group trigger: an explicit mention of the current WeChat account
    Direct trigger: every qualifying inbound direct message
    Outbound: GeWe postText REST endpoint over HTTPS
    Idempotency: appid plus newMsgId, as required by provider documentation
    Delivery control: serialized sends with at least one second between requests
    Failure domain: independent credentials, listener, health state, and queue
    Risk: WeChat may restrict or suspend accounts using third-party automation

All four channels normalize messages into the same persistent SQLite inbox.
They therefore share the same deduplication, rate limits, per-conversation
ordering, retry policy, dead-letter handling, Codex runtime, Persona, operating
Bible, conversation memory, Multica bridge, and audit trail.


ADDITIONAL CHANNEL SETUP

DingTalk

1. Install the official stable CLI:

       npm install -g dingtalk-workspace-cli@1.0.55

2. Authenticate the intended real-person account:

       dws auth login
       dws auth status --format json

3. If several DingTalk identities are stored, copy the stable profile selector
   returned by dws profile list into dingtalkProfile.

4. Set dingtalkEnabled to true in config.local.json, or request the same change
   through the dashboard Configuration Copilot and confirm the protected plan.

The minimum supported DWS version is 1.0.55 because AIPRO relies on the official
all-direct-message event user_im_message_receive_o2o_all. Group replies still
require a real @ mention and do not react to every ordinary group message.

WeCom

1. Create or select an enterprise WeCom AI Bot and obtain its Bot ID and Secret.

2. Set wecomBotId in config.local.json.

3. Open macOS Keychain Access and create a Generic Password:

       Account: the exact WeCom Bot ID
       Service: aipro-wecom-bot
       Password: the WeCom Bot Secret

   The service name can be changed through wecomKeychainService. The Secret is
   never stored in config.local.json, the browser, logs, snapshots, or Git.

4. Set wecomEnabled to true and restart AIPRO. The default endpoint is:

       wss://openws.work.weixin.qq.com

Personal WeChat through GeWe

1. In the GeWe console, create an API Token and log in the intended personal
   WeChat account. Copy the stable appId/device ID. Do not copy browser cookies
   or session storage into AIPRO.

2. Store the API Token in macOS Keychain as a Generic Password:

       Account: the exact GeWe appId
       Service: aipro-gewe
       Password: the GeWe API Token

   AIPRO automatically creates a separate random callback-path secret in the
   same Keychain service under the account `<appId>:callback`. Neither secret
   is stored in configuration files, dashboard responses, logs, snapshots, or
   Git.

3. Provide a stable public HTTPS reverse proxy or ingress. It must forward to:

       http://127.0.0.1:17656

   The local receiver never binds to the LAN. AIPRO appends its secret callback
   path to gewePublicCallbackBaseUrl and registers the resulting HTTPS URL with
   GeWe. A temporary tunnel is suitable only for testing; production requires a
   stable ingress with TLS, monitoring, and access logging that redacts paths.

4. Configure geweAppId and gewePublicCallbackBaseUrl, then set geweEnabled to
   true through config.local.json or the dashboard Configuration Copilot. If a
   GeWe callback v2 payload does not include explicit mentioned-account IDs,
   add the account display name to geweMentionNames. Callback v1 uses the exact
   atuserlist account ID and does not need this fallback.

5. Restart AIPRO. The channel is considered connected only when the loopback
   receiver is listening, the public callback is registered, and GeWe reports
   the WeChat account online. Any failure degrades only the personal WeChat
   card; it does not stop Feishu, DingTalk, WeCom, Codex, memory, or audit.

The local dashboard shows installed, configured, authenticated, connected, and
last-error states independently for Feishu, DingTalk, WeCom, and personal
WeChat.

Official channel sources:

    DingTalk Workspace CLI
    https://github.com/DingTalk-Real-AI/dingtalk-workspace-cli

    Tencent WeCom AI Bot Node.js SDK
    https://github.com/WecomTeam/aibot-node-sdk

    Tencent WeCom reference channel implementation
    https://github.com/WecomTeam/wecom-openclaw-plugin

Third-party personal WeChat provider documentation:

    GeWe Webhook events
    https://doc.geweapi.com/doc-3146208

    GeWe callback structures
    https://doc.geweapi.com/doc-3599831
    https://doc.geweapi.com/doc-8680561

    GeWe text sending
    https://doc.geweapi.com/api-139908313

    GeWe account-risk notice
    https://doc.geweapi.com/doc-6356339

This repository contains the complete single-Mac implementation. It is intended
for an account owner or an organization that has explicitly authorized the
account, application, permissions, and operating environment.


WHAT PROBLEM AIPRO SOLVES

Most enterprise chatbots operate as visible bot accounts. That model does not
fit every workflow. In some cases, colleagues already communicate with a real
person through existing group chats and direct messages. AIPRO adds an
automation layer behind that authorized identity while preserving the original
conversation context.

Typical use cases include:

    Answering routine questions in the account owner's communication style
    Responding when the owner is mentioned in a group
    Responding to direct messages sent to the owner
    Summarizing text, screenshots, documents, and meeting materials
    Drafting natural replies, reports, plans, and structured documents
    Generating a Word document and sending it to the original conversation
    Searching approved Feishu knowledge
    Checking accessible calendar information
    Preparing tasks or calendar events for owner confirmation
    Turning meeting notes into proposed action items
    Providing a visible health and maintenance interface

AIPRO does not authorize itself. It only operates within the permissions granted
by the account owner, the Feishu application, the operating system, lark-cli,
and the selected AI runtime.


CORE PRODUCT PRINCIPLES

1. Polling is the primary message source.

   AIPRO searches messages visible to the authorized user. This is the core
   path for group mentions and direct messages.

2. WebSocket is an auxiliary source.

   The real-time event consumer improves normal latency and provides an
   additional signal, but the system does not depend on WebSocket as its only
   source of incoming messages.

3. Both sources share one persistent inbox.

   Polling and WebSocket events are written into the same SQLite inbox. The
   Feishu message ID is the unique key, so one message is processed only once
   even when both sources deliver it.

4. Replies use the authorized user identity.

   Text and generated files are sent through official lark-cli user-identity
   commands. The repository does not implement unofficial account takeover or
   protocol reverse engineering.

5. Risk is evaluated before execution.

   Read-only work can run automatically. Actions that change real work state
   require a preview and confirmation from the account owner. Prohibited
   actions are refused.

6. Operations must be observable.

   Queue state, polling freshness, WebSocket state, SQLite integrity, process
   state, recent audit events, and recovery status are available through a
   local dashboard and health commands.


SUPPORTED MESSAGE BEHAVIOR

Group chats:

    All visible group chats can be enabled.
    AIPRO responds only when the real-person account is explicitly mentioned.
    A message sent by the account owner is ignored.
    A message sent by an application is ignored.
    Messages without a relevant mention are ignored.

Direct messages:

    Messages from other people to the account owner can trigger AIPRO directly.
    No mention is required in a direct message.
    Messages sent by the account owner are ignored.

Current default scope:

    allowAllChats is true.
    authorizedChatIds is an empty list.
    There is no chat whitelist.
    Group messages still require an explicit mention.
    Direct messages trigger directly.
    digitalTwinLabel is empty, so no fixed AI prefix is added.

Allowing all chats does not mean broadcasting to all chats. It means AIPRO may
respond in any visible conversation that satisfies the trigger rules. The
system does not automatically send unsolicited messages to every conversation.


END-TO-END MESSAGE FLOW

Step 1: Discover

    The polling loop searches group and direct messages in parallel.
    The WebSocket consumer listens for im.message.receive_v1 events.

Step 2: Filter

    The system removes deleted messages, messages from the owner, unsupported
    chat types, unsupported message types, non-mentioned group messages, and
    messages outside the configured scope.

Step 3: Persist

    Valid messages are inserted into a SQLite inbox. The message ID is unique.
    Duplicate delivery from another source is ignored.

Step 4: Schedule

    Messages in the same conversation are processed serially. Different
    conversations can be processed concurrently, up to the configured global
    limit.

Step 5: Decide

    AIPRO classifies the request, checks access rules, determines the risk level,
    and chooses direct execution, preview and confirmation, clarification, or
    refusal.

Step 6: Execute

    The selected AI runtime handles language understanding and response
    generation. Optional Feishu SDK functions handle documents, images, files,
    tasks, and calendars.

Step 7: Reply

    The final response is sent back to the original chat using the authorized
    user identity and an idempotency key.

Step 8: Record

    Conversation memory, workflow decisions, retries, confirmations, delivery
    results, and operational events are recorded locally.


MESSAGE POLLING DESIGN

The default polling interval is 5,000 milliseconds.

Each polling cycle:

    Searches group messages visible to the authorized user
    Restricts group results to messages that mention the owner
    Searches direct messages visible to the authorized user
    Requests all search-result pages
    Rejects incomplete pagination
    Normalizes results into the internal message format
    Inserts new messages into the persistent inbox
    Advances the polling cursor only after a complete successful search

Reliability windows:

    Default overlap: 3 minutes
    Initial baseline lookback: 15 minutes
    Default maximum catch-up period: 24 hours
    Default single search window: 15 minutes

The overlap protects against indexing delay and short network interruptions.
SQLite deduplication prevents the overlap from producing repeated replies.

On the first start, AIPRO creates a baseline from recent messages and marks them
as completed without replying. This prevents a fresh installation from replying
to old conversation history.

If the search API reports that more pages remain, AIPRO refuses to advance the
cursor. This favors a visible retry over silent message loss.


WEBSOCKET DESIGN

The default auxiliary transport is:

    lark-cli event consume im.message.receive_v1 --as bot

The consumer is supervised by the main process. When the consumer exits or
fails, AIPRO restarts it with bounded exponential backoff.

The alternative eventTransport value is sdk. In SDK mode, the Feishu Node SDK
opens the WebSocket connection directly. SDK mode requires an application
secret stored in the configured macOS Keychain service.

WebSocket availability is displayed separately from polling health. If
WebSocket is temporarily unavailable but polling remains healthy, AIPRO can
continue to receive qualifying user messages.


AI RUNTIME ABSTRACTION

AIPRO is not coupled to one AI coding CLI. It discovers supported local
runtimes during startup, selects one through a common execution adapter, and
uses that adapter for Feishu replies, configuration planning, action-item
extraction, and Multica request planning.

The default aiRuntime value is auto. Auto mode uses the first usable headless
runtime in this order:

    Codex CLI
    Qoder CLI
    CodeBuddy CLI

Current provider status:

    Codex CLI
        Production adapter available. Text and local image inputs are
        supported. AIPRO uses an ephemeral, isolated, read-only Codex session.

    Qoder CLI
        Production adapter available. The print-mode CLI is invoked with
        dont_ask permissions, an empty tool set, prompt input through stdin,
        and optional image attachments. Both current QoderWork application
        bundles and common user-level CLI locations are detected.

    CodeBuddy CLI
        Production text adapter available. The headless CLI is invoked with
        dontAsk permissions, text output, prompt input through stdin, bounded
        time, and bounded output. The provider becomes selectable only after a
        compatible local executable is detected and a real smoke test passes.

    TRAE
        The installed TRAE desktop application is detected and shown in the
        dashboard. It is not selectable in this release because its graphical
        launcher does not provide a stable headless response stream that AIPRO
        can safely supervise. Detection is not presented as execution support.

Installation requires no AIPRO-specific executable path when a supported CLI is
installed in a common location or available through PATH. Operators can keep
Auto mode or choose an available runtime in the dashboard. A runtime change is
a protected configuration transaction:

    Display the requested provider and exact configuration change
    Require a six-digit second confirmation
    Save a complete rollback snapshot
    Validate the resulting configuration
    Execute a real response smoke test with the selected runtime
    Restart the main service
    Verify service and channel health
    Restore the previous snapshot automatically if any step fails

The dashboard distinguishes three states: usable headless runtime, application
detected without a usable headless interface, and not installed. Executable
paths and authentication data are never returned to the browser.

Every AI subprocess uses:

    An isolated working directory
    A provider-specific noninteractive mode
    Prompts through stdin instead of command-line arguments
    Restricted or read-only execution policy where the provider supports it
    A bounded timeout
    Bounded standard output and standard error
    Controlled termination on timeout or service shutdown

Codex additionally uses an isolated Codex home, a link to the existing
authorized login, read-only sandbox mode, and no unrelated user configuration
or local skill loading.

Provider documentation used by this integration:

    CodeBuddy headless mode
    https://www.codebuddy.ai/docs/cli/headless

    CodeBuddy CLI reference
    https://www.codebuddy.ai/docs/cli/cli-reference

    Qoder CLI quick start
    https://docs.qoder.com/cli/quick-start

    Qoder permission modes
    https://docs.qoder.com/cli/permissions

    TRAE Agent open-source project
    https://github.com/bytedance/trae-agent

The prompt combines:

    The account owner's Persona
    The operating Bible
    The current workflow decision
    Recent conversation memory for the same chat and sender
    The current user request
    Authorized document or file content when applicable

The model is instructed not to read unrelated local files, run arbitrary
commands, fabricate authorization, claim personal approval, or execute
prohibited actions.


PERSONA AND OPERATING BIBLE

PERSONA.md defines:

    Professional identity
    Audience
    Expertise
    Tone
    Sentence style
    Preferred vocabulary
    Real de-identified writing samples
    Statements and behaviors to avoid

BIBLE.md defines:

    Trigger rules
    Workflows
    Risk levels
    Direct-execution actions
    Confirmation requirements
    Prohibited actions
    Human takeover behavior
    Completion criteria

The generated local Persona and Bible files are excluded from Git because they
may contain private operating context.


RISK LEVELS

Level L0: Direct execution

    Question answering
    Summarization
    Rewriting
    Translation
    Drafting
    Approved knowledge retrieval
    Image understanding
    File understanding

Level L1: Execute and report

    Reversible document generation
    Word report creation
    Deliverables returned to the original conversation

Level L2: Preview and owner confirmation

    Creating tasks
    Creating calendar events
    Batch creation of action items
    Cross-conversation delivery
    Publishing
    Formal submission
    Commitments that affect real work state

Level L3: Prohibited automatic execution

    Payments and transfers
    Contract signing
    Hiring commitments
    Legal or financial commitments
    Password, verification-code, or private-key handling
    Irreversible deletion
    Unauthorized identity use
    Concealing automation for a high-risk action

Only the configured ownerOpenId can confirm task, calendar, or batch action-item
creation. Other contacts may ask AIPRO to draft content, but they cannot use it
to perform owner-side mutations.

Multica reads remain available to legitimate contacts, but every general
Multica write requires a verified account Owner. The Owner check is repeated
when a write is previewed, when it is confirmed, and before an Issue is
executed. Other contacts cannot create, update, comment, assign, dispatch, or
execute an arbitrary Issue.

The only non-Owner write exception is constrained AIPRO feedback intake. For a
Bug, remediation comment, or feature request, AIPRO first asks one clarifying
acceptance question. After the answer it may create exactly one unassigned
`backlog` Issue containing the source channel, original conversation, original
request, clarification, and acceptance criteria. It subscribes the originating
conversation but never dispatches or executes that Issue. Owner feedback uses
the same clarification step, then a persistent outbox assigns the Issue to the
configured Owner Squad and moves it to `todo`.


TEXT, IMAGE, AND FILE CAPABILITIES

Text:

    General questions
    Summaries
    Rewriting and translation
    Draft replies
    Structured reports
    Operational commands

Images:

    Current-message images when the event contains a resource key
    Recent image lookup in the same conversation
    Up to four recent images by default
    Screenshot and visual-content understanding when the selected runtime
    supports image input

Files:

    PDF
    DOCX
    TXT
    Markdown source files
    CSV
    JSON
    LOG
    XML
    HTML

File limits:

    Maximum file size: 20 MB
    Maximum extracted text passed forward: 40,000 characters
    Maximum PDF pages inspected: 60

Recommended attachment workflow:

    First send the image or file.
    Then send a text instruction in the same conversation, such as:
    "Summarize the file I just sent."

AIPRO searches only the same conversation, only the same sender, and only the
previous 30 minutes. It does not substitute a file sent by another person.

Generated deliverables:

    AIPRO can produce a structured DOCX report.
    The file is written to the configured artifact directory.
    The file is sent back to the original conversation.

Python dependencies for file processing are pinned in requirements.txt and are
validated by npm run check.


FEISHU KNOWLEDGE

knowledge-catalog.json controls approved knowledge access for non-owner users.

Each catalog item may define:

    Document token
    Title
    URL
    Aliases
    Reader open IDs

The account owner may search documents visible to the configured Feishu
application. Other contacts can read only catalog items that explicitly include
their open ID.

Document content is truncated to the configured safe limit before it is passed
to the model. Responses are instructed to retain the source title and URL.


TASKS AND CALENDAR

AIPRO can prepare:

    A single Feishu task
    A batch of tasks extracted from meeting notes
    A calendar event
    A calendar query for today, tomorrow, or the day after tomorrow

Creation is not immediate.

    AIPRO parses the title and time.
    Missing required information is requested.
    A preview is sent.
    The owner replies with a confirmation command.
    Only then is the Feishu API called.

Pending confirmations are stored in SQLite and expire automatically.


MULTICA BUSINESS SYSTEM INTEGRATION

AIPRO includes a bidirectional Multica Issue adapter. It uses the official
Multica CLI and the currently authenticated Multica profile. AIPRO does not
read, copy, display, or send the Multica access token to Codex.

Supported Issue operations:

    Discover every workspace visible to the authenticated Multica account
    List Issues in one workspace
    Search Issue identifiers, titles, descriptions, and statuses
    Read a specific Issue by identifier or UUID
    Create an Issue
    Update title, description, status, priority, assignee, project, and dates
    Add a follow-up comment
    Follow or unfollow an Issue from the originating Feishu conversation
    Enable or stop all-workspace change synchronization for a conversation

There is no AIPRO Multica workspace allowlist. Every workspace returned by the
authenticated Multica account is available to the constrained planner. Multica
itself remains the authority for authentication and the account's effective
access.

Read operations execute immediately. Owner create, update, and comment
operations first return an exact preview and require Owner confirmation.
Cancellation and assignee changes require a six-digit second confirmation.
The Owner check is repeated at confirmation time. Delete, permission changes,
token operations, arbitrary API calls, arbitrary commands, and arbitrary
local-file access are not exposed.

Example Feishu requests:

    "查一下 MYS-2"
    "列出 Multica 里进行中的 Issues"
    "在黄山空间创建一个高优先级发布 Issue"
    "把 MYS-2 状态改为 in_review"
    "给 MYS-2 加一条跟进评论：等待客户确认"
    "关注 MYS-2 的变化"
    "把 Multica 全空间变化同步到这里"
    "停止向这里同步 Multica 全空间变化"
    "反馈一个 AIPRO Bug：状态更新没有同步回来"

Issue reads automatically associate the Issue with the current Feishu
conversation. Later changes to that Issue can therefore be sent back to the
same group or direct message. A conversation may also explicitly subscribe to
all-workspace synchronization so that newly created Issues and changes to any
visible Issue are reported there.

The synchronizer is polling-based and independent from Feishu message polling.
The default interval is 10 seconds. It scans all visible Multica workspaces,
stores a local fingerprint of relevant fields, and reports only changes after
the initial baseline. The baseline does not flood Feishu with existing Issues.
Notification idempotency keys prevent duplicate deliveries after retries or
restarts.

Commercial reliability controls include:

    Bounded pagination and a configurable maximum Issue count
    Bounded process time and output size
    Exponential retry for safe read operations
    No blind retry for non-idempotent Issue creation or comments
    Stale updated_at comparison before an Issue update
    Per-recipient notification isolation
    Persistent Issue cache and subscriptions in SQLite
    Baseline-on-first-start behavior
    Structured mutation and synchronization audit events
    Stable feedback registration keys and reconciliation before re-creation
    Persistent Owner-Squad dispatch outbox with bounded retry and dead letters
    Independent dashboard freshness and error indicators
    Read-only live smoke testing

If Multica is temporarily unavailable, Feishu message handling remains alive.
The Multica synchronization loop records the failure, uses bounded exponential
backoff, displays a degraded state, and resumes after the service recovers.


CONVERSATION MEMORY

Memory is isolated by:

    Chat ID
    Sender open ID

This prevents conversation context from one person from being mixed with
another person's context in the same group.

The model receives the most recent 12 memory entries. The database retains a
bounded history for each chat and sender. Scheduled maintenance removes expired
conversation and audit records according to the local retention policy.


PERSISTENT QUEUE AND DEDUPLICATION

The SQLite database contains:

    Conversation memory
    Settings and health state
    Audit events
    Persistent inbound messages
    Rate-limit counters
    Pending confirmations

Inbound states:

    pending
    processing
    failed
    completed
    dead

Claiming a message is an atomic database update. A second worker cannot claim
the same message at the same time.

Messages normally receive up to three processing attempts. Retry delays use
exponential backoff. If the final user-facing failure notice also cannot be
sent, the message enters the dead state so the original expensive operation is
not executed indefinitely.

When the service restarts, messages left in processing are returned to pending.
A singleton lock prevents two local main processes from running concurrently.


COMMERCIAL RELIABILITY CONTROLS

Polling backoff:

    AIPRO recognizes Feishu throttling responses separately from ordinary
    transient failures.
    A rate-limit response enters a minimum 60-second cooldown with jitter.
    Other polling failures never retry faster than the configured normal
    polling interval.
    The overlapping polling window and SQLite deduplication prevent the
    cooldown from silently losing qualifying messages.

External mutation journal:

    Confirmed Feishu task creation, calendar creation, and Multica writes use a
    persistent execution key derived from the confirmation message.
    The execution intent is committed to SQLite before the external write.
    A successful external result is stored before the user receipt is sent.
    Replayed confirmation processing returns the stored result without
    repeating the external write.
    If the process stops while the external outcome is unknown, AIPRO marks the
    operation ambiguous and blocks automatic replay. The operator is instructed
    to verify the target system before issuing a new request.
    This is an at-most-once safety policy. It favors a visible manual check over
    duplicate tasks, events, Issues, or comments.

Multica notification dead letters:

    Change notifications use a persistent outbox and deterministic delivery
    key.
    Delivery uses bounded exponential backoff.
    After ten failed attempts, a notification enters the dead state and is no
    longer retried automatically.
    Pending and dead notification counts are separate health signals.
    The dashboard and the owner status response show dead notifications as a
    maintenance condition.

Database durability and backup:

    SQLite runs in WAL mode with synchronous FULL.
    Maintenance creates a transactionally consistent online backup at startup
    and every six hours.
    Every backup is opened read-only and checked with PRAGMA quick_check before
    it is accepted.
    Fourteen rolling backup files are retained with owner-only permissions.
    A stale or failed backup changes dashboard health to degraded.
    npm run backup-smoke opens the newest backup and validates its integrity and
    required commercial-reliability tables.

Truthful dependency health:

    Executable discovery alone does not prove an AI provider is healthy.
    AIPRO records the latest real AI runtime success and failure.
    When the latest real call failed, the dashboard reports a degraded runtime
    even if the process and executable are still present.


RATE LIMITING

The default non-owner rate limit is:

    10 triggering messages
    Per sender
    Per 5-minute window

The owner is not subject to this inbound limit.

When a user exceeds the limit, AIPRO sends at most one rate-limit notice during
the same window. This prevents notification amplification.


HUMAN TAKEOVER

The account owner can send the localized pause or resume command defined by the
deployed command parser. The exact production command strings are maintained in
src/operator-commands.mjs and src/index.mjs.

The pause state is stored per conversation. While paused, AIPRO records that a
message was skipped and does not generate an automated reply.


OPERATOR COMMANDS

The deployed command parser supports four operator actions:

    Status
    Help
    Pause automated handling
    Resume automated handling

The account owner receives a detailed status response. Other users receive a
reduced view that does not expose internal queue or local process details.


LOCAL OPERATIONS DASHBOARD

Dashboard URL:

http://127.0.0.1:17655

The dashboard is an independent local watchdog. It listens only on the loopback
interface and is not exposed to the local network or the public internet.

The dashboard displays:

    Main process state and PID
    Process start time
    Polling freshness
    Last successful poll
    Poll duration
    Last polling error
    WebSocket state
    Active WebSocket consumer count
    Selected AI runtime and auto-discovery state
    Availability of Codex, Qoder, CodeBuddy, and the detected TRAE application
    AI runtime proxy reachability when configured
    SQLite integrity
    Pending, processing, failed, completed, and dead counts
    Recent sanitized audit events
    Current scope configuration
    Current event transport
    Current model
    Multica integration state and last synchronization freshness
    Last Multica scan, change, and notification counts
    A conversational configuration assistant
    Before-and-after change previews
    Confirmation level and confirmation code
    Recent configuration rollback points
    Click-to-configure controls for every IM channel
    Per-channel connection tests with visible pass/fail checks

The dashboard refreshes every five seconds.

It can restart the main LaunchAgent. The restart endpoint verifies the request
host, origin, and a required custom action header.

IM CHANNEL SETUP AND CONNECTION TESTS

Each IM channel card is a configuration entry point. Clicking a card opens a
local modal that displays only the fields required by that channel. The
dashboard automatically reuses installed CLIs and existing local login state
where possible. Operators do not need to edit config.local.json for routine
channel setup.

The Feishu card is protected: it can be inspected and connection-tested but
cannot be disabled or overwritten from this workflow. DingTalk exposes its DWS
profile selector. WeCom accepts the official Bot ID and Bot Secret. Personal
WeChat accepts the GeWe App ID, API Token, public HTTPS callback URL, and local
mention names.

Secrets entered in the local modal are sent only to the loopback dashboard and
stored directly in macOS Keychain. They are never returned to the browser,
written to config.local.json, appended to audit logs, or committed to Git. The
Keychain command reads secret material from standard input so it does not appear
in the process argument list. An existing secret is represented only by a
boolean "stored" state.

Saving a channel performs a complete transaction:

    Validate required fields and safe URL rules
    Store or reuse the channel credential in macOS Keychain
    Create a configuration snapshot
    Validate the generated configuration
    Restart the main process
    Verify the main Feishu polling, WebSocket, and SQLite health
    Verify the selected channel runtime, configuration, authentication, and connection
    Restore both configuration and the prior Keychain credential on failure

The modal also provides a read-only "test current connection" action. It shows
each validation check separately so an operator can distinguish missing local
runtime, missing configuration, authentication failure, and transport failure.
Disabled optional channels report a healthy disabled state and do not degrade
the Feishu + Codex primary path.

CONVERSATIONAL CONFIGURATION ASSISTANT

The dashboard includes a local conversation window for operators who do not
want to edit JSON or policy documents by hand. An operator can describe the
desired behavior in natural language, such as changing the polling interval,
making replies shorter, or strengthening an approval rule.

This is a constrained configuration planner, not an unrestricted shell. The
selected AI runtime runs in a bounded planning session and can propose changes
only to an explicit set of configuration fields and operating documents. It
cannot change application credentials, the owner identity, Keychain settings,
binary paths, the dashboard port, proxy settings, or arbitrary files. The
assistant rejects credential-like input.

The assistant follows this transaction:

    Read the current safe configuration and behavior documents
    Produce a typed change plan
    Validate every proposed field against a local allowlist
    Display the exact before and after values
    Require operator confirmation
    Require a six-digit second confirmation for high-impact changes
    Create a complete local snapshot
    Write files atomically
    Validate the resulting configuration
    Restart the main service
    Check the process, polling, WebSocket, and SQLite health
    Automatically restore the snapshot if validation or health checks fail
    Append a metadata-only audit record

High-impact changes include conversation scope, event transport, AI runtime,
Codex model, the Operating Bible, and the knowledge catalog. Plans expire after
fifteen minutes and can be applied only once. A stale plan is rejected when its
original configuration has changed before confirmation.

The local browser session receives a random in-memory session token. Mutating
requests require the correct loopback Host, HTTP Origin, action name, and
session token. The dashboard does not listen on the LAN or public internet.

Recent snapshots are available in the same panel. Manual rollback also creates
a safety snapshot, restarts the service, and verifies health. If a rollback
attempt fails, AIPRO tries to restore the immediately preceding state.

Local configuration snapshots:

    data/config-backups/

Configuration audit log:

    data/configuration-audit.jsonl

The dashboard sets restrictive browser security headers, disables caching,
denies framing, and uses a Content Security Policy.

macOS notifications are produced for:

    Channel failure
    Partial recovery
    Full recovery


SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS

Operating system:

    macOS 13 or later

Node.js:

    Version 22.5.0 or later
    AIPRO uses the built-in node:sqlite module

Python:

    Python 3.10 or later
    python-docx 1.2.0
    pypdf 6.10.0

Package manager:

    pnpm is recommended
    npm is supported as a fallback

AI runtime:

    At least one supported headless CLI must be installed and authenticated
    Supported selectable runtimes are Codex, Qoder, and CodeBuddy
    Auto mode prefers Codex, then Qoder, then CodeBuddy
    The ChatGPT macOS application can provide the Codex executable
    QoderWork application bundles can provide the Qoder executable
    A detected TRAE desktop app is visible but is not selectable

Feishu runtime:

    Official @larksuite/cli
    An authorized Feishu custom application
    User OAuth permissions
    Optional application secret for SDK business capabilities

Multica runtime:

    Multica desktop application with its official CLI
    An authenticated Multica profile
    At least one workspace visible to that profile


INSTALLATION

1. Install the official Feishu CLI.

    pnpm add -g @larksuite/cli

   Or:

    npm install -g @larksuite/cli

2. Clone the repository.

    git clone https://github.com/James19890801/feishu-codex-digital-employee.git
    cd feishu-codex-digital-employee

3. Run setup.

    chmod +x scripts/*.sh
    ./scripts/setup.sh

The setup script:

    Checks Node.js
    Checks Python
    Detects Codex, Qoder, CodeBuddy, and TRAE
    Requires at least one supported headless AI runtime
    Checks lark-cli
    Installs locked Node dependencies
    Installs pinned Python dependencies
    Creates config.local.json
    Creates PERSONA.md
    Creates BIBLE.md
    Creates the local data directory


FEISHU USER OAUTH

Required user OAuth scopes:

    offline_access
    search:message
    im:message
    im:message:readonly
    im:message.group_msg:get_as_user
    im:message.p2p_msg:get_as_user
    im:message.send_as_user

Login command:

    lark-cli auth login --scope 'offline_access search:message im:message im:message:readonly im:message.group_msg:get_as_user im:message.p2p_msg:get_as_user im:message.send_as_user'

Verification command:

    lark-cli auth check --scope 'search:message im:message im:message:readonly im:message.group_msg:get_as_user im:message.p2p_msg:get_as_user im:message.send_as_user' --json

A successful result must report ok as true and must not report missing required
scopes.


FEISHU APPLICATION SETUP

The Feishu custom application must:

    Be owned or managed by an authorized person or organization
    Be available to the account owner
    Have an approved and published application version
    Subscribe to im.message.receive_v1 for auxiliary events
    Have message permissions required by the selected identity mode
    Have additional document, resource, task, or calendar permissions for
    optional business capabilities

Permission names and approval requirements may vary by Feishu tenant and
application configuration. A permission appearing in the developer console is
not enough; the application version must be published and the resource must be
visible to the calling identity.


APPLICATION SECRET STORAGE

Core message polling and user-identity replies use user OAuth and do not require
an application secret in the repository.

The following capabilities require SDK application credentials:

    Direct SDK WebSocket mode
    Image resource download
    File resource download
    Feishu document access
    Task operations
    Calendar operations

Store the application secret in macOS Keychain:

    security add-generic-password -U \
      -a 'cli_YOUR_APP_ID' \
      -s 'codex-feishu-digital-employee' \
      -w 'YOUR_APP_SECRET'

Never place the secret in config.local.json, source code, logs, the README, or a
Git commit.

If lark-cli background processes report that Keychain access is blocked, the
following command changes lark-cli credential storage behavior:

    lark-cli config keychain-downgrade

Use that command only after accepting its security impact. It may allow
processes under the same macOS user to read a local credential file.


LOCAL CONFIGURATION

The primary local configuration file is config.local.json.

Minimal example:

    {
      "feishuAppId": "cli_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx",
      "ownerOpenId": "ou_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx",
      "authorizedChatIds": [],
      "allowAllChats": true,
      "digitalTwinLabel": "",
      "eventTransport": "lark-cli",
      "aiRuntime": "auto",
      "pollIntervalMs": 5000,
      "maxConcurrentReplies": 2,
      "multicaEnabled": true,
      "multicaProfile": "desktop-api.multica.ai",
      "multicaDefaultWorkspaceId": "",
      "multicaOwnerSquad": "詹老师的开发团伙",
      "multicaSyncIntervalMs": 10000,
      "multicaMaxIssues": 5000,
      "dashboardPort": 17655,
      "codexModel": "gpt-5.6-terra"
    }

Identity and scope fields:

    feishuAppId
        Required Feishu custom application ID.

    ownerOpenId
        Required open ID of the real-person account being assisted.

    allowAllChats
        Enables all qualifying visible conversations when true.

    authorizedChatIds
        Chat allowlist used when allowAllChats is false.

    digitalTwinLabel
        Optional text added before replies. An empty string adds no prefix.

    actionItemDocumentToken
        Optional document token used for meeting-note action-item extraction.

Polling and reliability fields:

    eventTransport
        lark-cli or sdk.

    pollIntervalMs
        Default 5000.

    pollOverlapMs
        Default 180000.

    pollInitialLookbackMs
        Default 900000.

    pollMaxCatchupMs
        Default 86400000.

    pollWindowMs
        Default 900000.

    maxConcurrentReplies
        Default 2. Allowed range 1 through 4.

    rateLimitWindowMs
        Default 300000.

    rateLimitMaxMessages
        Default 10.

Multica fields:

    multicaEnabled
        Enables the Multica Issue adapter and background synchronizer.

    multicaProfile
        Authenticated Multica CLI profile name.

    multicaDefaultWorkspaceId
        Optional UUID used when a create request does not name a workspace.

    multicaOwnerSquad
        Squad name used only for verified Owner feedback dispatch. Defaults to
        "詹老师的开发团伙". Non-Owner feedback never uses this value.

    multicaSyncIntervalMs
        Default 10000. Allowed range 5000 through 300000.

    multicaMaxIssues
        Maximum Issues scanned across all workspaces. Default 5000.

    multicaBin
        Optional official Multica CLI executable path. The default points to
        the CLI bundled with Multica.app.

Timeout and runtime fields:

    aiRuntime
        auto, codex, qoder, codebuddy, or trae. Auto is the default. An
        explicitly selected provider must expose a supported headless CLI.
        TRAE is reserved for a future supervised headless adapter and is
        rejected on systems that only have the desktop application.

    larkCliTimeoutMs
        Default 45000.

    codexTimeoutMs
        Default 120000.

    helperTimeoutMs
        Default 30000.

    dashboardPort
        Default 17655.

    artifactDir
        Optional output directory for generated files.

    codexBin
        Optional Codex executable override. Normal installations are discovered
        automatically.

    codexModel
        Codex model name. It is ignored when another runtime is selected.

    codexProxyUrl
        Optional trusted HTTP or HTTPS proxy.

    larkCli
        Optional lark-cli executable path.

    nodeBin
        Optional Node.js binary directory.

    pythonBin
        Optional Python interpreter path.

All integer settings have minimum and maximum validation. Invalid App IDs, open
IDs, paths, ports, intervals, concurrency values, proxy protocols, and transport
values fail during startup instead of allowing a partially broken process.


STARTING THE SERVICES

Install and start the main service:

    ./scripts/install-service.sh

Install and start the dashboard:

    ./scripts/install-dashboard-service.sh

Run full verification:

    ./scripts/verify.sh

The main LaunchAgent label is:

    com.local.feishu-codex-digital-employee

The dashboard LaunchAgent label is:

    com.local.feishu-codex-dashboard

Both services use RunAtLoad and KeepAlive. They are automatically restarted by
macOS when the process exits unexpectedly.


OPERATIONS COMMANDS

Check the main service:

    launchctl print "gui/$(id -u)/com.local.feishu-codex-digital-employee"

Check the dashboard service:

    launchctl print "gui/$(id -u)/com.local.feishu-codex-dashboard"

Restart the main service:

    launchctl kickstart -k "gui/$(id -u)/com.local.feishu-codex-digital-employee"

Watch main logs:

    tail -f bridge.log bridge-error.log

Watch dashboard logs:

    tail -f dashboard.log dashboard-error.log

Run the local health check:

    npm run health

Check the event consumer:

    npm run event-health

Run a real sample with the configured AI runtime:

    npm run runtime-smoke

Validate the latest restorable state database backup:

    npm run backup-smoke

Run an explicit Codex-only sample:

    npm run codex-smoke

Run a read-only live Multica sample:

    npm run multica-smoke

Run the complete acceptance suite:

    ./scripts/verify.sh


