    config.local.json
    PERSONA.md
    BIBLE.md
    data/
    node_modules/
    log files
    Python cache files
    macOS metadata files

The data directory may contain:

    SQLite state
    SQLite WAL files
    Process locks
    Dashboard notification state
    Isolated AI runtime files
    Local Codex authentication link when Codex is used

Do not upload the data directory.


SECURITY AND PRIVACY

Credential controls:

    No application secret is stored in source code.
    User OAuth is managed by official lark-cli.
    Optional SDK secrets are stored in macOS Keychain.
    Local configuration and runtime data are ignored by Git.

AI runtime controls:

    Runtime availability checked before selection
    Real smoke test before a runtime configuration is committed
    Automatic rollback after failed selection or health verification
    Provider-specific noninteractive and restricted execution
    Prompts delivered through stdin
    Isolated working directory
    Isolated Codex home when Codex is used
    Bounded time and output
    Controlled process termination

Message controls:

    Sender validation
    Chat-type validation
    Group mention requirement
    Owner-message exclusion
    Message-ID deduplication
    Per-sender rate limiting
    Owner-only mutation confirmation

Dashboard controls:

    Loopback-only listening
    Host validation
    Origin validation for every mutating or connection-test request
    Random in-memory dashboard session token
    Required custom action header
    Content Security Policy
    No caching
    Frame denial
    Protected Feishu primary-channel configuration
    Secret-only Keychain writes with no credential echo

Data controls:

    Bounded memory per conversation
    Scheduled retention cleanup
    Sanitized dashboard event details
    SQLite integrity checks
    WAL checkpoint maintenance

No security control can make an authorized real-person automation system
risk-free. The account owner remains responsible for authorization, disclosure,
governance, access review, and human intervention.


KNOWN LIMITATIONS

1. Single-machine availability

   The current version runs on one Mac. It cannot respond while that Mac is
   powered off, sleeping, logged out, disconnected, or physically damaged.

2. No fixed end-to-end latency guarantee

   Response time depends on polling, Feishu indexing, network conditions, AI
   runtime inference, optional resource downloads, and message delivery.

3. Upstream service dependency

   Feishu, lark-cli, the selected AI runtime, its upstream model service, the
   local network, and optional proxy services can fail independently.

4. Maximum catch-up period

   The default configuration catches up no more than 24 hours after an outage.

5. Optional capability credentials

   Text polling and user-identity sending may be healthy while media, document,
   task, or calendar capabilities are unavailable because of missing
   application permissions or SDK credentials.

6. node:sqlite status

   The current Node.js runtime may print an experimental warning for
   node:sqlite. The warning itself does not mean the database is unhealthy.

7. No multi-node failover

   Achieving infrastructure-level high availability requires a hosted queue,
   shared database, leader election, multiple workers, remote monitoring, and a
   continuously available deployment environment. Those features are outside
   this single-Mac release.

8. Governance responsibility

   An empty digitalTwinLabel removes a fixed display prefix. It must not be used
   to justify deception, unauthorized identity use, or high-risk commitments.


TROUBLESHOOTING

Problem: A group mention receives no reply.

    Confirm that ownerOpenId belongs to the real-person account.
    Confirm that the group message explicitly mentions that account.
    Check user OAuth scopes.
    Run npm run health.
    Inspect bridge-error.log.

Problem: A direct message receives no reply.

    Confirm that the message was sent by another person.
    Confirm p2p user-message permission.
    Confirm that the conversation is not paused.
    Check polling freshness.

Problem: A message is received but the selected AI runtime does not answer.

    Run npm run runtime-smoke.
    Confirm that the selected runtime is authenticated.
    Open the dashboard and inspect the AI runtime cards.
    Switch to Auto or another usable runtime through the protected workflow.
    Check codexProxyUrl when a proxy is required.
    Inspect timeout and process errors.

Problem: Text works but images, files, tasks, or calendars do not work.

    Check the application secret in macOS Keychain.
    Check published Feishu application permissions.
    Run npm run check for Python dependencies.
    Confirm that the resource is visible to the application.
    Confirm that the owner is the person approving a mutation.

Problem: The dashboard does not open.

    Check the dashboard LaunchAgent.
    Run:

    curl --fail http://127.0.0.1:17655/api/status

    Inspect dashboard-error.log.

Problem: WebSocket is disconnected.

    Run npm run event-health.
    Run:

    lark-cli event status --json --fail-on-orphan

    If polling remains healthy, the primary message path may continue operating
    while the auxiliary event consumer recovers.

Problem: Keychain access is blocked.

    Review the security implications first.
    If accepted, run:

    lark-cli config keychain-downgrade

    Restart the main service and verify again.


